  takeawayParagraphs:[
    'Geography changes the possibilities people face. Water, soils, terrain, and available materials affect the effort needed for different activities. Yet the same environment can support several workable responses. A river might sustain irrigation, fishing, transport, or combinations of these. Explaining a society therefore requires connecting a physical condition with an actual choice. We must also explain why people favored that choice over another possibility. Environmental evidence narrows an explanation without supplying the entire answer.',
    'Knowledge develops through observation, experiment, teaching, and repeated practice. A resource becomes useful through skills that people acquire and share. Technical solutions also depend on how communities coordinate effort and evaluate results. Different traditions can solve similar problems in different ways. Historical comparison should examine these processes instead of treating a familiar development name as evidence of identical achievement. A convincing explanation connects what people knew with what they were able to organize.',
    'Institutions and relationships shape which possibilities become realities. Rules about authority, ownership, and exchange influence access to resources and the distribution of benefits. Connections with other communities can provide knowledge and supplies while creating new obligations or risks. Conflict, negotiation, and cooperation can change the direction of development. Geography, technology, and social choices therefore belong in the same explanation. Compare societies through evidence about their particular circumstances, rather than placing them on a single ladder of progress.'
  ]
